>> title: Ginger-Scallion Oil
>> category: Sauces & Gravies
>> tags: ginger, scallion, chinese, condiment, vegan
>> counters: Dim Sum Counter
>> aka: ginger scallion oil, ginger scallion sauce, geung chung yau, jiang cong you, 薑蔥蓉, ginger and spring onion relish
>> pairs-with: white-cut-chicken
>> servings: 8
>> time: 30 min
>> step.1: mince fine, salt, mound it in a heatproof bowl
>> step.2: heat the oil to smoking, pour it over in one go
>> step.3: stir the sesame oil through, rest 20 min

Mince @fresh ginger{2%oz}(55 g; peeled) and @scallions{6}(white and green, sliced thin) as fine as your patience allows, and toss them with @kosher salt{1%tsp} in a #heatproof bowl{}.

Heat @peanut oil{1/2%cup}(120 mL) in a #saucepan{} until it shimmers and just begins to smoke, then pour it over @&(~1)ginger and scallion{} in one go — it should hiss and climb the bowl. Oil poured on warm leaves you a bowl of raw onion, which is the one way this goes wrong.

Stir @toasted sesame oil{1%tsp} and @white pepper{1/4%tsp} into @&(~1)relish{} and ~rest{20%min} before it goes to the table. It keeps a week in the fridge and it belongs beside white cut chicken, plain rice, and anything poached.
